pe":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"wrong\",\"rationale\":\"The answer gets only one of the four categories fully right and invents two others. Correct: the §22(e) delayed foreign-delivery relief, including 'no longer than necessary' and the 15-day cap. Partly correct: it names §17(a)(1)–(2) relief for in-kind basket transactions. But it misstates the key limit. The rule covers only persons who are affiliated solely because they hold 5% (or more than 25%) of the ETF's voting securities, or are affiliates of such holders. The answer omits this and substitutes an unsupported basket-selection limit. Missing: the redeemable-security treatment under §2(a)(32) despite creation-unit dealing, and the relief to trade shares on exchanges at market prices (§22(d)/Rule 22c-1). Fabricated: §17(d)/Rule 17d-1 joint-transaction relief and §24(d) prospectus-delivery relief, neither of which Rule 6c-11(b) provides. The central list of four categories is therefore largely false.\"}","ttft_ms":3688,"type":"result","duration_ms":7099,"uuid":"9a51653b-8343-4cf1-85aa-023dceb5b6ab","ttft_stream_ms":567,"time_to_request_ms":69,"first_content_frame_ms":567,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
